  "assistantTitle": "Vera Assistant",
  "assistantInputHint": "Ask about your cycle…",
  "assistantIntro": "Hi! I can answer questions about periods, cycles, and how Vera works — right here on your phone, nothing leaves your device. I'm not a doctor and this isn't medical advice.",
  "insightMenstrual3": "Period blood isn't \"dirty blood\" — it's a mix of blood and uterine lining, a completely normal part of the cycle.",
  "insightMenstrual4": "Gentle heat on the lower belly genuinely eases cramps — a warm water bottle is an evidence-backed classic.",
  "insightMenstrual5": "Flow is often heaviest in the first two days and tapers off after — that pattern is typical.",
  "insightMenstrual6": "Feeling a bit more tired during your period is common — iron leaves the body with menstrual blood.",
  "insightFollicular3": "Estrogen is rebuilding your uterine lining after your period — your body is prepping fresh.",
  "insightFollicular4": "Skin often looks its clearest in this phase as hormone levels settle.",
  "insightFollicular5": "Endurance and reaction times peak in the late follicular phase for many people.",
  "insightFollicular6": "This phase's length is what varies most between people — the luteal phase is far more constant.",
  "insightFertile3": "An egg lives about 12–24 hours, but sperm can wait up to 5 days — that's why the fertile window spans several days.",
  "insightFertile4": "Around ovulation, cervical mucus often turns clear and stretchy — like raw egg white.",
  "insightFertile5": "Some people feel a brief one-sided twinge at ovulation — it even has a name: mittelschmerz.",
  "insightFertile6": "Basal body temperature rises slightly *after* ovulation — it confirms it happened rather than predicting it.",
  "insightLuteal3": "Progesterone peaks about a week after ovulation — bloating and breast tenderness around then are common.",
  "insightLuteal4": "Craving carbs before your period is real — energy needs rise slightly in the luteal phase.",
  "insightLuteal5": "PMS symptoms usually ease within a day or two after bleeding starts.",
  "insightLuteal6": "A consistent sleep routine can noticeably soften late-luteal mood dips.",
  "insightUnknown2": "Tracking just three cycles is often enough to see your own pattern emerge.",
  "insightUnknown3": "Cycles are personal — comparing yours to a friend's rarely tells you anything useful.",
  "insightUnknown4": "Stress, travel, and illness can all shift ovulation — a \"late\" period often just means late ovulation.",
